---
/**
 * Squares where a section's top rule crosses the page rails.
 *
 * The blueprint doctrine — *a grid marks every crossing of its own rules, or
 * none of them* — applied at page scale rather than inside a single grid. Every
 * section boundary on a framed page crosses both rails, so every one of them
 * gets a pair.
 *
 * Drop it as the first child of any section that carries `border-t border-line`
 * inside `.page-rails`. **The host must be `relative`** — without it the squares
 * resolve against the nearest positioned ancestor and land somewhere else
 * entirely, which is silent and looks like a spacing bug.
 *
 * Not a pseudo-element: `[data-blueprint]` already owns `::before` on these
 * sections and `[data-dead]` owns `::after`, and a square needs both a surface
 * fill (to punch through the rule) and its own border, which one pseudo-element
 * cannot carry anyway.
 */
---

<span class="bp-sq bp-sq--t bp-sq--rail-l" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<span class="bp-sq bp-sq--t bp-sq--rail-r" aria-hidden="true"></span>
